---
import Work from '../../layouts/Work.astro';
import FlowDiagram from '../../components/FlowDiagram.astro';
---

<Work
  title="AI WordPress Publisher"
  category="AI · Automation · SEO"
  summary="A configurable Python pipeline that turns keyword briefs into SEO articles and publishes them through the WordPress REST API on a controlled schedule."
  description="LLM content pipeline: keyword briefs in, scheduled WordPress articles with SEO metadata out. Python, LLM APIs and the WordPress REST API."
  year="2026"
  role="Sole author — architecture and implementation"
  status="Active, running in production"
  stack={['Python', 'LLM APIs', 'WordPress REST API', 'Yoast SEO', 'openpyxl']}
  links={[{ label: 'View on GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/Takeshhii/ai-wordpress-publisher' }]}
>
  <FlowDiagram
    caption="Pipeline architecture — brief in, scheduled post out"
    steps={[
      { label: 'Config & briefs', note: 'Per-site keyword briefs' },
      { label: 'LLM generation', note: 'Article + SEO metadata' },
      { label: 'Validation', note: 'De-dup, structure checks' },
      { label: 'Scheduler', note: 'Paced publishing slots' },
      { label: 'WordPress API', note: 'Publishes on schedule' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    I run two content-dependent properties — <a href="/work/aira">AIRA</a> and an
    <a href="/work/nfc-msk">NFC products business</a> — and neither has a content team.
    Producing and scheduling SEO articles by hand does not scale past a handful a week.
  </p>
  <p>
    The obvious shortcut makes things worse. Generating everything and publishing it in
    one batch produces a site that reads as machine-dumped rather than maintained — a
    weak signal for readers and search engines alike. The pacing is not a cosmetic
    detail; it is part of the requirement.
  </p>

  <h2>The system</h2>
  <p>
    A Python pipeline that takes a CSV of keyword briefs and produces fully-formed,
    scheduled WordPress posts with no manual step in between.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Briefs</strong> — one row per article: main keyword, secondary keywords,
      search intent, an H2/H3 outline, internal-link targets and a target length.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Generation</strong> — the brief is expanded into a full article plus
      Yoast SEO title, meta description and focus keyword.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Publishing</strong> — posts go through the WordPress REST API using a
      scoped application password, created with <code>post_status: future</code> so
      WordPress's own scheduler releases them. The pipeline does not need to be running
      when a post goes live.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pacing</strong> — each site has its own daily limit and publish-time
      slots; the next open slot is chosen automatically with a random offset so timing
      does not look scripted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resumability</strong> — every generated article is cached with its
      publish state, and anything already published for a keyword is skipped. The run
      can be stopped, resumed weeks later, or given a second batch of briefs without
      producing duplicates.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Handling pre-written content</h2>
  <p>
    A second path exists for articles that arrive already written, usually as
    spreadsheet exports. Those exports are frequently templated — dozens of articles
    built on one paragraph skeleton with the keyword swapped — which is a
    near-duplicate content risk if published as-is. That path rewrites the templated
    passages while preserving facts and structure, de-duplicates against everything
    already live, and schedules the result through the same logic.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    Sole author. I designed the brief schema, the generation step, the WordPress
    integration, the scheduling and pacing logic, and the de-duplication and resume
    behaviour. The manual-import path was added after running into templated exports
    in practice.
  </p>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    Two content operations now run from one shared configuration instead of being
    managed by hand. The pipeline has been used to plan and schedule 100 articles
    across both sites from keyword briefs alone, at a publishing pace that stays
    deliberately human.
  </p>

  <h2>Lessons</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The scheduling logic is a small part of the codebase and the part that decides
      whether the output is perceived as credible. Automation quality is not only
      about the generated text.
    </li>
    <li>
      Making a pipeline resumable and de-duplicated from the start is what makes it
      safe to actually use. Without it, every re-run is a risk.
    </li>
    <li>
      Getting SEO metadata to write cleanly through the REST API took experimentation —
      worth documenting, because it is the kind of integration detail that quietly
      costs hours.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Work>
